      <div type="clause" n="El. 49">
        <head><ref type="lawCode" target="law:Af">Af</ref> El. 49</head>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="ang" source="#liebermann" n="El. 49" corresp="#wit-e"><pb n="42" ed="#liebermann"/>Þis sindan<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="8" target="#Af_El-to-49.lnote.oe.8"/> ða domas þe se ælmihtega<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="9" target="#Af_El-to-49.lnote.oe.9"/> God self sprecende<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="10" target="#Af_El-to-49.lnote.oe.10"/> wæs to Moyse ⁊ him bebead<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="11" target="#Af_El-to-49.lnote.oe.11"/> to healdanne; ⁊ siððan<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="12" target="#Af_El-to-49.lnote.oe.12"/> se áncenneda Dryhtnes<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="13" target="#Af_El-to-49.lnote.oe.13"/> sunu, ure God, þæt is hælend Crist, on middangeard cwom<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="14" target="#Af_El-to-49.lnote.oe.14"/>, he cwæð, ðæt he ne come<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="15" target="#Af_El-to-49.lnote.oe.15"/> no<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="16" target="#Af_El-to-49.lnote.oe.16"/> ðas bebodu to brecanne<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="17" target="#Af_El-to-49.lnote.oe.17"/> ne to forbeodanne, ac mid eallum godu<hi rend="italic">m</hi> to ecanne<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="18" target="#Af_El-to-49.lnote.oe.18"/>; ⁊ mildheortnesse<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="19" target="#Af_El-to-49.lnote.oe.19"/> ⁊ eaðmodnesse<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="19" target="#Af_El-to-49.lnote.oe.19"/> he lærde.</ab>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="ang" source="#liebermann" n="El. 49" corresp="#wit-g-prol-only"><pb n="42" ed="#liebermann"/>[Ð]is syndan ða domas ðe se ælmihtiga God sylf sprecende wæs to Moyse ⁊ him bebead to healdende<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="1" target="#Af_El-to-49.lnote.oe.1"/>; ⁊ syððan se acenneda Drihtnes sunu, úre God, þ<hi rend="italic">æt</hi> ys hælende Crist, on middangeard cóm, he cwæð, þ<hi rend="italic">æt</hi> he ne come ná þas bebodu to brecanne ne to forbeodanne, ac mid eallu<hi rend="italic">m</hi> godum to icanne; ⁊ mildheortnesse ⁊ eaðmodnesse he lærde.</ab>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="ang" source="#liebermann" n="El. 49" corresp="#wit-h"><pb n="43" ed="#liebermann"/>Ðis sindon ða domas ðe se ælmihtiga God sylf<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="7" target="#Af_El-to-49.lnote.oe.7"/> sprecende wæs to Moyse ond<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="2" target="#Af_El-to-49.lnote.oe.2"/> him bebead to healdenne; ond<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="2" target="#Af_El-to-49.lnote.oe.2"/> siððan se acenneda Godes sunu, ðæt is hælende Crist, on woruld becom, he cwæð, þæt he ne come no<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="2" target="#Af_El-to-49.lnote.oe.2"/> þas (word <hi rend="italic">uel<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="8" target="#Af_El-to-49.lnote.oe.8-2"/></hi>) bebodu to brecanne ne to forbeodanne, ac mid eallum godum to geecenne<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="9" target="#Af_El-to-49.lnote.oe.9-2"/>; ond<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="10" target="#Af_El-to-49.lnote.oe.10-2"/> mildheortnesse ond<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="2" target="#Af_El-to-49.lnote.oe.2"/> eaðmodnesse<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="11" target="#Af_El-to-49.lnote.oe.11-2"/> he lærde.</ab>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="la" source="#liebermann" n="El. 49" corresp="#wit-q"><ptr type="liebermannNote" n="4" target="#Af_El-to-49.lnote.oe.4"/><pb n="43" ed="#liebermann"/>Hæc sunt iudicia quæ Dominus ipse locutus est ad Moysen et precepit ei<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="5" target="#Af_El-to-49.lnote.oe.5"/> custodire. Et postquam<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="6" target="#Af_El-to-49.lnote.oe.6"/> natus pro salute<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="7" target="#Af_El-to-49.lnote.oe.7-2"/> nostra Dei filius, dominus et saluator noster Jesus Christus, uenit in mundum, ait: ‘Non <ptr type="liebermannNote" n="*" target="#Af_El-to-49.lnote.oe.star"/>ueni legem<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="8" target="#Af_El-to-49.lnote.oe.8-3"/> soluere sed adimplere’<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="9" target="#Af_El-to-49.lnote.oe.9-3"/>, pietate scilicet<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="10" target="#Af_El-to-49.lnote.oe.10-3"/> et<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="11" target="#Af_El-to-49.lnote.oe.11-3"/> misericordia.</ab>
        <ab type="editionText" xml:lang="ang" source="#jurasinski-and-oliver" n="MP 49" corresp="#wit-e"><pb n="262" ed="#jurasinski-and-oliver"/>Þis sindan ða domas þe se ælmihtega God self sprecende wæs to Moyse ⁊ him bebead to healdanne; ⁊ siððan se áncenneda Dryhtnes sunu, ure God, þæt is hælend Crist, on middangeard cwom, he cwæð, ðæt he ne come no ðas bebodu to brecanne ne to forbeodanne, ac mid eallum godu<hi rend="italic">m</hi> to ecanne; ⁊ mildheortnesse ⁊ eaðmodnesse he lærde.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="de" source="#liebermann"><pb n="43" ed="#liebermann"/>Dies [Obige] sind die Gebote, welche der allmächtige Gott<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="1" target="#Af_El-to-49.lnote.de.1"/> selbst zu Moses sprach<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="2" target="#Af_El-to-49.lnote.de.2"/> und ihm gebot zu halten.<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="3" target="#Af_El-to-49.lnote.de.3"/> Und als der eingeborene<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="4" target="#Af_El-to-49.lnote.de.4"/> Sohn des Herrn, unser Gott, das ist Heiland Christus, zur Erde<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="5" target="#Af_El-to-49.lnote.de.5"/> kam, sagte er, dass er nicht gekommen sei, diese Gebote zu brechen oder zu verbieten, sondern mit allem Guten<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="6" target="#Af_El-to-49.lnote.de.6"/> zu vermehren: und<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="7" target="#Af_El-to-49.lnote.de.7"/> Barmherzigkeit und Demuth lehrte er.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude" ana="#fromGerman">These [above] are the commandments which almighty God himself spoke to Moses and commanded him to keep. And when the only-begotten Son of the Lord, our God, that is Christ the Saviour, came to earth, he said that he had not come to break these commandments or to forbid them, but to increase them with all good things: and he taught mercy and humility.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#jurasinski-and-oliver" corresp="#wit-e"><pb n="263" ed="#jurasinski-and-oliver"/>These are the judgments that almighty God was speaking himself to Moses and bade him keep; and after the only begotten son of the Lord our God, that is the savior Christ, came into the world, he declared, that he did not come in any way to break or forbid these commandments, but rather to increase them with all good [laws]; and he taught mercy and humility.</ab>
        <note type="passageSource">Exodus 23,13; Matthew 5,17</note>
        <note type="comparison" source="#liebermann" n="43">Matt. 5.17</note>
        <note type="comparisonPrinted" source="#liebermann">[Matt. 5, 17]</note>
        <note type="comparison" source="#jurasinski-and-oliver" n="263"><label>same idiom (ecan, 'to supplement')</label> <ref target="clause:Hl_Prolog">Hl Prol.</ref> <quote>Alfred makes use of an idiom earlier employed by Hloðhere and Eadric, Wihtred, and Ine in the prologues to their respective codes</quote></note>
        <note type="comparison" source="#jurasinski-and-oliver" n="263"><label>same idiom (ecan, 'to supplement')</label> <ref target="clause:Wi_Prolog-to-3">Wi Prol.</ref> <quote>Alfred makes use of an idiom earlier employed by Hloðhere and Eadric, Wihtred, and Ine in the prologues to their respective codes</quote></note>
        <note type="comparison" source="#jurasinski-and-oliver" n="263"><label>same idiom (ecan, 'to supplement')</label> <ref target="clause:Ine_Ine-Prol">Ine Prol.</ref> <quote>Alfred makes use of an idiom earlier employed by Hloðhere and Eadric, Wihtred, and Ine in the prologues to their respective codes</quote></note>
