        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Af_El-to-49.lnote.oe.10-2" n="10" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">43</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">d <hi rend="italic">übergeschr.,</hi> o <hi rend="italic">geänd. in</hi> a</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">d written above, o altered to a</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Af_El-to-49.lnote.oe.11-2" n="11" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">43</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">geänd. in</hi> eadm.</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">altered to eadm.</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Af_El-to-49.lnote.oe.4" n="4" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">43</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Item de eodem <hi rend="italic">roth R</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">Item de eodem in red R</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Af_El-to-49.lnote.oe.5" n="5" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">43</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">ea <hi rend="italic">T</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">ea T</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Af_El-to-49.lnote.oe.6" n="6" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">43</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">hier beginnt Br</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">Br begins here</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Af_El-to-49.lnote.oe.7-2" n="7" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">43</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">n. s. <hi rend="italic">Co</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">n. s. Co</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Af_El-to-49.lnote.oe.star" n="*" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">43</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">Wo Quadr. mehr Matthaeus und den Actus als Af folgt, setze ich</hi> {m} <hi rend="italic">und</hi> {a}.</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">Where Quadr. follows Matthew and the Acts more closely than it follows Af, I put {m} and {a}.</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Af_El-to-49.lnote.oe.8-3" n="8" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">43</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">fehlt Br</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">missing in Br</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Af_El-to-49.lnote.oe.9-3" n="9" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">43</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">impl. <hi rend="italic">Dm</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">impl. Dm</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Af_El-to-49.lnote.oe.10-3" n="10" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">43</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">sua <hi rend="italic">T;</hi> sola <hi rend="italic">Hk Br</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">sua T; sola Hk Br</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Af_El-to-49.lnote.oe.11-3" n="11" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">43</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">ac <hi rend="italic">R; übergeschr.</hi> ac <hi rend="italic">Dm</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">ac R; ac written above Dm</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Af_El-to-49.lnote.oe.1" n="1" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">42</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">so</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">sic</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Af_El-to-49.lnote.de.1" n="1" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">47</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">3</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">S. o.</hi> Pro². 11¹</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude"><hi rend="italic">See above</hi> Pro². 11¹</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Af_El-to-49.lnote.de.2" n="2" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">47</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">3</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Die periphrastische Konstruktion bedeutet damals noch nicht Dauer der Handlung</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">At that date the periphrastic construction does not yet signify duration of the action</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Af_El-to-49.lnote.de.3" n="3" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">48</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">1</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">healdenne, -nde</hi> in HG sind spätere Sprache; Sievers <hi rend="italic">Gram.</hi> § 363</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude"><hi rend="italic">healdenne, -nde</hi> in HG are later language; Sievers <hi rend="italic">Gram.</hi> § 363</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Af_El-to-49.lnote.de.4" n="4" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">48</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">1</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Nur E’s Lesung bed. <hi rend="italic">unigenitus;</hi> GH nur ‘erzeugt’. — ‘Gottes Sohn’ H</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">Only E’s reading means <hi rend="italic">unigenitus;</hi> GH only ‘begotten’. — ‘God’s Son’ H</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Af_El-to-49.lnote.de.5" n="5" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">48</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">1</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">‘Welt’ HQ modernisierend, <hi rend="italic">vgl. Wb: middangeard.</hi> — Die Matthäusstelle zitiert auch Ælfric <hi rend="italic">Homil.</hi> II 198. — Die Korrektur in H zu ‘Gebote’ ‘Worte oder’ will entweder den Mosaismus nicht als Gebot für Christen gelten lassen oder nur bibelgenau dem Texte Ex. 20, 1 näher bleiben</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">‘world’ HQ, modernizing; <hi rend="italic">cf. Wb: middangeard.</hi> — The passage from Matthew is also quoted by Ælfric <hi rend="italic">Homil.</hi> II 198. — The correction in H at ‘commandments’, ‘words or’, is meant either to keep the Mosaic law from counting as a commandment for Christians, or merely, with biblical exactness, to stay closer to the text of Ex. 20, 1</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Af_El-to-49.lnote.de.6" n="6" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">48</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">1</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">‘allen guten Gesetzen’ Turk <hi rend="italic">Legal code</hi> 31. Aber Christi Lehre, bes. Barmherzigkeit und Demut, nannte Af schwerlich ‘Gesetze’. <hi rend="italic">God</hi> substantiviert: ‘Gutheit, Edelsinn’</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">‘all good laws’ Turk <hi rend="italic">Legal code</hi> 31. But Af would hardly have called Christ’s teaching, esp. mercy and humility, ‘laws’. <hi rend="italic">God</hi> used as a noun: ‘goodness, nobility of mind’</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Af_El-to-49.lnote.de.7" n="7" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">48</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">1</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">= ‘nämlich, also’. — Die christliche Verbesserung besteht laut Ælfred im Dispens von Jüdischen Riten (El 49, 7) und im milden Strafrecht (El 49, 9). Jener Satz 49, 7 bildet den Kern des Briefes von Fulk von Reims, der wohl zu diesem Abschnitte anregte; <hi rend="italic">s.</hi> Einl. 24</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">= ‘namely, thus’. — According to Ælfred, the Christian improvement consists in dispensation from Jewish rites (El 49, 7) and in a mild criminal law (El 49, 9). That sentence, 49, 7, forms the core of the letter of Fulk of Reims, which probably prompted this section; <hi rend="italic">see</hi> Introduction 24</seg></note>
      </div>
